he progenitor cells lying at the anterior surface of the lens. In response to various signals, these cells elongate and migrate towards the posterior pole, while simultaneously undergoing a process of denucleation and degradation of cellular organelles. This unique process ensures that the cells do not scatter light, which is critical for the transparency of the lens. After reaching full maturity, lens fiber cells form tightly packed layers, known as laminae, to make up the distinctive architecture of the ocular lens. \n\n  Line 523: The primary function of basal cells involves stem cell activity and epithelial tissue maintenance. Basal cells in the trachea play a crucial role in the regeneration and repair of the tracheal epithelium during damage or injury, acting as progenitor cells for ciliated and secretory cells. They serve as a reserve pool of cells, ready to proliferate and differentiate as needed for homeostatic tissue maintenance or in repair processes when the epithelium has been compromised, whether by injury or disease.\n\n  Line 765: CL:0000129\tCL:0000129\tCL_0000129\t\"Microglial cells, sometimes referred to as microglia, are a type of glial cell that primarily exist within the central nervous system (CNS), notably in the brain and the spinal cord. Classified among the resident immune cells, microglial cells represent about 10% of all cells found within the CNS. These cells are derived from progenitor cells in the yolk sac, which differentiates them from other types of glial cells (such as astrocytes and oligodendrocytes) that are derived from neuroectodermal cell lineages. \n\n  Line 805: CL:0000557\tCL:0000557\tCL_0000557\t\"Granulocyte monocyte progenitor cells (GMP cells), originating from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow, are a critical intermediate in myeloid cell development. Positioned at a key differentiation juncture, these cells exhibit bidirectional potential, giving rise to both granulocyte and monocyte lineages.\n\n  Line 808: CL:0007010\tCL:0007010\tCL_0007010\t\"Preosteoblast is a critical cell type involved in bone formation, crucial in a process known as ossification or osteogenesis. They are derived from mesenchymal stem cells and are an intermediate stage marking the transition from an osteoprogenitor cell to a fully differentiated osteoblast. The presence of preosteoblasts signifies the site of new bone deposition, highlighting their crucial role in skeletal development and in response to bone injury repairs.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/subsets/kidney_upper_slim.tsv:\n  Line 13: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000038\terythroid progenitor cell\n\n  Line 17: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000050\tmegakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor cell\n\n  Line 61: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000553\tmegakaryocyte progenitor cell\n\n  Line 76: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000837\thematopoietic multipotent progenitor cell\n\n  Line 77: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000839\tmyeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell\n\n  Line 79: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002031\thematopoietic lineage restricted progenitor cell\n\n  Line 80: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002032\thematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell\n\n  Line 107: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0011026\tprogenitor cell\n\n  Line 164: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0002244\thematopoietic progenitor cell differentiation\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/subsets/kidney_upper_slim.owl:\n  Line 2802:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A relatively undifferentiated cell that retains the ability to divide and proliferate throughout life to provide progenitor cells that can differentiate into specialized cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 2820:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A relatively undifferentiated cell that retains the ability to divide and proliferate throughout life to provide progenitor cells that can differentiate into specialized cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 3043:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A progenitor cell committed to the erythroid lineage.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 3056:         <rdfs:label>erythroid progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 3057:         <skos:prefLabel>erythroid progenitor cell</skos:prefLabel>\n\n  Line 3068:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A progenitor cell committed to the erythroid lineage.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 3186:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A progenitor cell committed to myeloid lineage, including the megakaryocyte and erythroid lineages.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 3215:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A progenitor cell committed to myeloid lineage, including the megakaryocyte and erythroid lineages.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 3307:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A progenitor cell committed to the megakaryocyte and erythroid lineages.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 3316:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>megakaryocyte/erythroid progenitor cell</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 3322:         <rdfs:label>megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 3323:         <skos:prefLabel>megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor cell</skos:prefLabel>\n\n  Line 3334:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A progenitor cell committed to the megakaryocyte and erythroid lineages.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 3853:         <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mesenchymal progenitor cells</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\n\n  Line 3920:         <owl:annotatedTarget>mesenchymal progenitor cells</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 5131:         <obo:IAO_0000115>An immature, nucleated erythrocyte occupying the stage of erythropoeisis that follows formation of erythroid progenitor cells. This cell is CD71-positive, has both a nucleus and a nucleolus, and lacks hematopoeitic lineage markers.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 5151:         <owl:annotatedTarget>An immature, nucleated erythrocyte occupying the stage of erythropoeisis that follows formation of erythroid progenitor cells. This cell is CD71-positive, has both a nucleus and a nucleolus, and lacks hematopoeitic lineage markers.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 5641:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>megacaryocyte progenitor cell</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 5643:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>megakaryocytic progenitor cell</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 5651:         <rdfs:label>megakaryocyte progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 5689:         <owl:annotatedTarget>megakaryocytic progenitor cell</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 6848:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A hematopoietic multipotent progenitor cell is multipotent, but not capable of long-term self-renewal. These cells are characterized as lacking lineage cell surface markers and being CD34-positive in both mice and humans.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 6854:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>hemopoietic progenitor cell</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 6861:         <rdfs:label>hematopoietic multipotent progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 6862:         <skos:prefLabel>hematopoietic multipotent progenitor cell</skos:prefLabel>\n\n  Line 6867:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A hematopoietic multipotent progenitor cell is multipotent, but not capable of long-term self-renewal. These cells are characterized as lacking lineage cell surface markers and being CD34-positive in both mice and humans.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 6903:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A progenitor cell restricted to the myeloid lineage.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 6905:         <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>myeloid progenitor cell</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>\n\n  Line 6915:         <rdfs:label>myeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 6916:         <skos:prefLabel>myeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell</skos:prefLabel>\n\n  Line 6927:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A progenitor cell restricted to the myeloid lineage.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 7007:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A hematopoietic progenitor cell that is capable of developing into only one lineage of hematopoietic cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 7013:         <rdfs:label>hematopoietic lineage restricted progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 7014:         <skos:prefLabel>hematopoietic lineage restricted progenitor cell</skos:prefLabel>\n\n  Line 7019:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A hematopoietic progenitor cell that is capable of developing into only one lineage of hematopoietic cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 7055:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell that has the ability to differentiate into limited cell types but lacks lineage cell markers and self renewal capabilities.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 7062:         <rdfs:comment>This cell type is intended to be compatible with any vertebrate hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell.  For mammalian hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cells known to be lineage-negative, please use the term &apos;hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell&apos; (CL_0001060).</rdfs:comment>\n\n  Line 7063:         <rdfs:label>hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 7064:         <skos:prefLabel>hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell</skos:prefLabel>\n\n  Line 7069:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell that has the ability to differentiate into limited cell types but lacks lineage cell markers and self renewal capabilities.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 8191:         <rdfs:label>progenitor cell</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 8192:         <skos:prefLabel>progenitor cell</skos:prefLabel>\n\n  Line 10576:         <obo:IAO_0000115>The process in which precursor cell type acquires the specialized features of a hematopoietic progenitor cell, a class of cell types including myeloid progenitor cells and lymphoid progenitor cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 10577:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>haematopoietic progenitor cell differentiation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 10578:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>haemopoietic progenitor cell differentiation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 10579:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>hemopoietic progenitor cell differentiation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 10582:         <rdfs:label>hematopoietic progenitor cell differentiation</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 10587:         <owl:annotatedTarget>The process in which precursor cell type acquires the specialized features of a hematopoietic progenitor cell, a class of cell types including myeloid progenitor cells and lymphoid progenitor cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 21699:         <rdfs:comment>Less potent than multipotent, often thought of as precursor or progenitor cell status.</rdfs:comment>\n\n  Line 22538:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A protein that is a translation product of the human CD34 gene or a 1:1 ortholog thereof. It is a leukocyte membrane protein expressed specifically by lymphohematopoietic progenitor cells. It contains a single-pass transmembrane domain and that show distinct expression on early hematopoietic precursors and vascular-associated tissue. Acts as a scaffold that presents selectin carbohydrate ligands in a clustered, tissue specific manner to allow for higher avidity interactions between leukocytes and endothelial cells during the inflammatory process. In common with related sialomucins (endoglycan and podocalyxin), the extracellular region is dominated by an N-terminal mucin-like domain, which is densely substituted with sialylated O-linked carbohydrates. The mucin-like region is followed by a cysteine-containing and presumably globular domain. This domain may fold into an immunoglobulin-like structure as the positions of 2 of the cysteines are conserved in the C2 set of the immunoglobulin superfamily. The cytoplasmic domain is around 73-76 residues long and highly conserved.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 22541:         <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>hematopoietic progenitor cell antigen CD34</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n\n  Line 22550:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A protein that is a translation product of the human CD34 gene or a 1:1 ortholog thereof. It is a leukocyte membrane protein expressed specifically by lymphohematopoietic progenitor cells. It contains a single-pass transmembrane domain and that show distinct expression on early hematopoietic precursors and vascular-associated tissue. Acts as a scaffold that presents selectin carbohydrate ligands in a clustered, tissue specific manner to allow for higher avidity interactions between leukocytes and endothelial cells during the inflammatory process. In common with related sialomucins (endoglycan and podocalyxin), the extracellular region is dominated by an N-terminal mucin-like domain, which is densely substituted with sialylated O-linked carbohydrates. The mucin-like region is followed by a cysteine-containing and presumably globular domain. This domain may fold into an immunoglobulin-like structure as the positions of 2 of the cysteines are conserved in the C2 set of the immunoglobulin superfamily. The cytoplasmic domain is around 73-76 residues long and highly conserved.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 41645:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A cone-like structure that is formed when myocardial progenitor cells of the heart field fuse at the midline. The heart rudiment is the first structure of the heart tube.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 41658:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A cone-like structure that is formed when myocardial progenitor cells of the heart field fuse at the midline. The heart rudiment is the first structure of the heart tube.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/subsets/kidney_upper_slim.obo:\n  Line 80: def: \"A relatively undifferentiated cell that retains the ability to divide and proliferate throughout life to provide progenitor cells that can differentiate into specialized cells.\" [GOC:tfm, MESH:D013234]\n\n  Line 122: is_a: CL:0011026 ! progenitor cell\n\n  Line 136: intersection_of: RO:0002215 GO:0002244 ! capable of hematopoietic progenitor cell differentiation\n\n  Line 151: relationship: RO:0002215 GO:0002244 ! capable of hematopoietic progenitor cell differentiation\n\n  Line 157: name: erythroid progenitor cell\n\n  Line 158: def: \"A progenitor cell committed to the erythroid lineage.\" [GOC:add, ISBN:0721601464]\n\n  Line 172: is_a: CL:0000839 {is_inferred=\"true\"} ! myeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell\n\n  Line 173: relationship: RO:0002202 CL:0000050 ! develops from megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor cell\n\n\n(Results truncated: showing 100 of 2921 matches (2821 hidden). Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)","metadata":{"matches":2921,"truncated":true},"title":"progenitor cell","time":{"start":1778554106627,"end":1778554106816}},"id":"prt_e1a15f0060011jcna1x7RLoRuc","sessionID":"ses_1e5ec234fffeUkEJ1HQmwClUGD","messageID":"msg_e1a146c55001Rd2z89Z1Vu7wEi"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778554107798,"sessionID":"ses_1e5ec234fffeUkEJ1HQmwClUGD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8b3e1010865d6dd2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"fallopian tube epithelium"},"output":"Found 6 matches\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 40:       \"body\": \"**Updates on text and logical defs (have been reviewed by @biobenkj ):**\\n\\n**label**\\ndual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell\\n\\n**textual definition**\\n\\nA bipotent progenitor cell within the human fallopian tube epithelium, characterized by the concurrent expression of epithelial (e.g., EpCAM/CD326) and endothelial (e.g., PECAM1/CD31) markers at both the cell surface and transcript levels. This cell has the capacity to differentiate into ciliated and secretory epithelial cells, as well as potentially endothelial/stromal lineages. Positioned at the apex of lineage bifurcation, the cell exhibits stem-like and endothelial features, representing an intermediate developmental state between undifferentiated progenitors and lineage-committed epithelial cells.\\n\\n PMID: [40475517](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40475517/)\\n\\n**Synonyms**\\n\\nnon-ciliated secretory epithelial cell 2-1 (NCSE2-1 cells) - related synonym -  PMID: [35320732](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35320732/)\\nnon-ciliated secretory epithelial cell 2-2 (NCSE2-2 cells) - related synonym -  PMID: [35320732](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35320732/)\\nunclassified fallopian tube progenitors - exact-  PMID: [40475517](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40475517/)\\n\\n**Logical definition:**\\n\\n'progenitor cell'\\n'part of' some 'fallopian tube epithelium'\\n'in taxon' some 'Homo sapiens'\\n'develops into' some 'fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell'\\n'develops into' some 'fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell'\\n\",\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl:\n  Line 31229: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2013.10.013\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030006 \"The other cell types of the fallopian tube epithelium include the ciliated cell and the intercalary cell, or peg cell. The fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell type has been implicated as a source of high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma.\")\n\n  Line 31974: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2210508\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30023181\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033014 \"A small, narrow, peg-shaped epithelial cell with little cytoplasm that is part of oviduct epithelium. This cell is rarer than the ciliated and secretory epithelial cells of the fallopian tube epithelium and is often found intercalated between them. Peg cells are generally distributed basally along the epithelium and have been found in high concentrations at the fimbriated, distal end of the fallopian tube in humans. It may have a regenerative/stem-cell function. In humans, markers include EPCAM, CD44, and ITGA6.\")\n\n  Line 34317: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:40475517\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052070 \"A bipotent progenitor cell within the human fallopian tube epithelium, characterized by the concurrent expression of epithelial (e.g., EpCAM/CD326) and endothelial (e.g., PECAM1/CD31) markers at both the cell surface and transcript levels. This cell has the capacity to differentiate into ciliated and secretory epithelial cells, as well as potentially endothelial/stromal lineages. Positioned at the apex of lineage bifurcation, the cell exhibits stem-like and endothelial features, representing an intermediate developmental state between undifferentiated progenitors and lineage-committed epithelial cells.\")\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl:\n  Line 31228: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2013.10.013\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030006 \"The other cell types of the fallopian tube epithelium include the ciliated cell and the intercalary cell, or peg cell. The fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell type has been implicated as a source of high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma.\")\n\n  Line 31973: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2210508\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30023181\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033014 \"A small, narrow, peg-shaped epithelial cell with little cytoplasm that is part of oviduct epithelium. This cell is rarer than the ciliated and secretory epithelial cells of the fallopian tube epithelium and is often found intercalated between them. Peg cells are generally distributed basally along the epithelium and have been found in high concentrations at the fimbriated, distal end of the fallopian tube in humans. It may have a regenerative/stem-cell function. In humans, markers include EPCAM, CD44, and ITGA6.\")\n","metadata":{"matches":6,"truncated":false},"title":"fallopian tube epithelium","time":{"start":1778554107764,"end":1778554107796}},"id":"prt_e1a15f491001PYI5wNawULD3du","sessionID":"ses_1e5ec234fffeUkEJ1HQmwClUGD","messageID":"msg_e1a146c55001Rd2z89Z1Vu7wEi"}}
